.TH DYNBLK 8 "2026-09-17" "Dynblk 1.0.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
dynblk \- manage compressed native and split VMDK block devices
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dynblk create PATH
.RB [ --format " dynblk|vmdk" ]
.RB [ --size " SIZE" ]
.RB [ --compression " CODEC" ]
.RB [ --part-size " SIZE" ]
.RB [ --subformat " twoGbMaxExtentSparse" ]
.RB [ --cache " MODE" ]
.RB [ --map-memory-mb " N" ]
.RB [ --module " FILE" ]
.RB [ --execute ]
.br
.B dynblk load PATH
.RB [ --format " auto|dynblk|vmdk" ]
.RB [ --cache " MODE" ]
.RB [ --map-memory-mb " N" ]
.RB [ --read-only ]
.RB [ --module " FILE" ]
.RB [ --execute ]
.br
.B dynblk status /dev/dynblkN
.RB [ --json ]
.br
.B dynblk grow /dev/dynblkN SIZE
.RB [ --execute ]
.br
.B dynblk unload /dev/dynblkN
.RB [ --execute ]
.br
.B dynblk inspect PATH --metadata-only
.RB [ --json ]
.br
.B dynblk check PATH
.RB [ --json ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
DynBlk exposes file-backed disks through /dev/dynblkN on Linux 6.12.
Native format 1 provides compression and split sparse storage. The VMDK
backend supports twoGbMaxExtentSparse without parents or compression.
No daemon, FUSE or QEMU runtime is required. CLI commands never create or
resize the filesystem inside the device.
.PP
Native images use the DBSPRS01 layout with format number 1.
Unsupported layouts are rejected. Install matching CLI and module versions.
.SH COMMANDS
Create exclusively creates and attaches a new container; load opens existing
storage and normally detects its format. Both print the assigned device.
Grow takes an absolute capacity, never shrinks, and does not resize the inner
filesystem. Unload uses the attachment cookie and refuses a device still in use.
Status reports format, effective cache policy, memory accounting and capabilities.
Inspect scans mapping metadata; check also reads mapped payloads and checks
native compressed CRCs and decoding. Neither repairs storage.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --execute
Perform create/load/grow/unload instead of preview. Requires root.
.TP
.B --format
Create: dynblk (default) or vmdk. Load: auto (default), dynblk or vmdk.
.TP
.B --cache
writeback (default), writethrough, none, directsync or unsafe. This is a
per-attachment setting, not an on-disk policy. None bypasses the host page
cache but still honors flushes; it does not mean synchronous writes.
Direct modes currently require ext2/ext4 backing storage. Writethrough and
directsync synchronize every ordinary write. Unsafe deliberately omits
persistence barriers and can corrupt the image after a crash.
.TP
.B --map-memory-mb
Metadata-page cache budget, 1..64 MiB; default 1 MiB. Not a fraction of RAM
and not a cap on mapped data. Scratch buffers and host page cache are additional.
.TP
.B --size
Create-only capacity, default 16GiB.
Use dynblk limits --format dynblk --json for the native geometry limit:
standard geometry permits 64TiB with the current 65536-part resource guard.
Split VMDK additionally has a descriptor-size limit below 1MiB.
Integer bytes or
KiB/MiB/GiB/TiB suffixes, positive and divisible by 4096.
.TP
.B --part-size
Native physical file cap, 1MiB..4000MiB (default 4000MiB), 4096-aligned.
Smaller caps also reduce logical span and maximum capacity with 65536 parts.
.TP
.B dynblk limits [--format dynblk|vmdk] [--part-size SIZE] [--json]
Report implementation geometry limits without root, a device or module loading.
VMDK descriptor length, open-file limits and available resources may impose
lower practical limits. Native disk format remains 1.
.TP
.B --subformat
VMDK creation supports only twoGbMaxExtentSparse.
.TP
.B --compression
Native-only codec: none (default), lz4, lz4hc, lzo, lzo-rle, zstd, deflate or 842.
The corresponding kernel provider must be available. VMDK requires none.
.TP
.B --read-only
Load without modifying backing storage, including recovery. Growth and writes
are rejected. Read-only mounts are available through mount.dynblk as well.
.TP
.B --module
Use an existing, trusted absolute path to dynblk.ko; otherwise use modprobe.
.TP
.B --json
Machine-readable status/inspection result, including effective cache and format.
.SH PATHS AND LIMITATIONS
Use canonical absolute paths without symlink components and single-link regular
files. Backing paths may be user-owned or user-writable, including FAT/exFAT
mounts with synthetic ownership and permissions. Primary basenames contain only
letters, digits, dots, underscores and hyphens; sibling paths must not escape the
directory. Writable attachments hold exclusive cooperative locks; readers use
shared locks. A user who can write the backing directory can still corrupt,
rename or delete the image; external writers that ignore locks are unsupported.
.PP
Native compressed partial writes currently recompress a 64-KiB grain. Native
and VMDK discard unmap wholly freed grains and reuse storage. On ext4, freed
file blocks can be punched automatically; other backing filesystems only trim
free tails. Live relocation requires an explicit reclaim --compact command. Raw data has no stored checksum; full
check validates its readability, not every possible corruption. Mapping metadata
is not journaled. See FORMAT.md for synchronization ordering and limits.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mount.dynblk (8), mount (8), umount (8)

.SH RECLAIM
.B dynblk reclaim /dev/dynblkN [--compact] [--scan-zeroes] [--max-steps N] [--execute] [--json]
.br
By default, return unreferenced ext4 file blocks and truncate free tails without
moving live data. On exFAT and other filesystems without hole punching, only
free tails are truncated. Automatic discard handling never relocates live data.
.PP
--compact explicitly enables online in-place relocation into free earlier runs.
It is never an automatic fallback. No format conversion or second image is used.
--scan-zeroes additionally reads mapped grains to find wholly zero payloads.
The command does not issue filesystem FITRIM; run fstrim on the inner filesystem
first when discovering deleted files is needed. Do not use the merged live root.
.PP
Without --execute, no device is opened. With --max-steps, complete:false in JSON
means the bounded pass stopped early. Safe completed steps remain in place.
Read-only and unsafe-cache attachments reject reclamation. Punch-range and
truncated-length counters are not measurements of physical allocated-byte savings.
